## 1. Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) [1–3], constructed by metal centers and organic ligands, have attracted extensive attention in many fields, such as catalysis [4–7], sensor [8–10], gas absorption and separation [11–13]. Recently, MOFs have emerged as popular electrode materials for supercapacitors because of their unique
structures and functionality [14–18]. Their metal ions with variable valence state can be employed as pseudocapacitive redox sites, enabling fast surface faradic redox reactions [19–22]. The large specific surface area of MOFs facilitates electrolyte penetration and charge storage [23–25]. However, the sluggish electron and ion transport in most MOF electrodes limit the utilization of the active sites during redox reactions, leading to low electrochemical activity in aqueous electrolytes. To mitigate this issue, micro/-nanostructuring of MOF-based electrode materials has been proposed as an effective strategy that can effectively shorten ions diffusion length [26–29].
Nickel-based materials exhibit promising electrochemical properties and have been regarded as potential electrode materials for supercapacitors [30–33]. However, nickel-based materials can deliver high specific capacitance but with unfavorable rate capability and cycling life, which may be attributed to their poor structural stability during the fast charge–discharge process. Introducing a secondary metal ion, especially \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\), can not only provide more active sites and improve the electrical conductivity, but also stabilize the nickel species [34–38]. Hence, fabrication of bimetallic cobalt/nickel-based materials is an available method to boost the supercapacitor performance.
Based on the above discussion, we present a strategy to improve the supercapacitor performance of monometallic Ni-MOF by \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) substitution. By taking Ni-MOF [39] \(\left(\{\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{tdc})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}\}_{\mathrm{n}}\right.\), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{tdc}=2,5\)-thiophenedicarboxylic acid) as a prototypical model, we demonstrate that \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) substitution in Ni-MOF (Co/Ni-MOFs) is able to effectively tune the intrinsic reactivity and electrical conductivity. Compared to Ni-MOF, the bimetallic Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 with an optimized Co:Ni ratio exhibits excellent electrochemical performance with higher specific capacitance and better rate performance and cycling stability. In addition, the asymmetric supercapacitor constructed with Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 and activated carbon shows a high specific capacitance with \(228\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at a current density of \(0.5\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and excellent cycling stability with \(95.5\,\%\) of capacitance retention after 5000 circulations at \(5\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\).
## 2. Results and discussion
## 2.1. Synthesis and structural characterization
Ni-MOF and the partially substituted Co/Ni-MOF-x:y (x:y represents the molar ratio of \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) added in the initial synthesis of Co/Ni-MOFs, rather than the actual ratio of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\), x:y = 1:2, 1:1, 2:1, and 3:1) were prepared from solvothermal reactions according to the literature method [39]. The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the obtained MOFs match well with the simulated XRD pattern of \(\{\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(-\mathrm{tdc})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}\}_{\mathrm{n}}\) (Ni-MOF-simulated) [39] (Fig. 1a and Figure S1), which consists of nickel octahedral \([\mathrm{Ni}10_{6}]_{2}[\mathrm{Ni}20_{6}]\) chains arranging parallel to the a axis that are further pillared by the bis(bidentate) \(\mathrm{tdc}^{2-}\) ligands to form a 3D framework (Fig. 1b). It is worth mentioning when the molar ratio of \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was greater than or equal to 5:1, another structure of \(\{\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{TDC})\) \((\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{1.5}\}_{\mathrm{n}}\) (Co-MOF-simulated) (Figure S2) was formed [40,41], in which 1D chains of octahedral \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) dimers are arranged in layers parallel to the \((b,\ c)\) planes and further communicate with one another in the a axis by \(\mathrm{tdc}^{2-}\) anions (Figure S3). This result
demonstrates that there is a coordination competition between \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{tdc}^{2-}\) ligands and the amount of \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) has a significant impact on the final structures. Fourier-transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra of Ni-MOF and Co/Ni-MOF-x:y exhibit similar characteristic peaks (Figure S4), suggesting that the as-synthesized MOFs possess identical functional groups. The adsorption peak at \(3597\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is attributed to the elongation of \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\). The peaks at \(1572\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1364\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) correspond to the antisymmetrical and symmetrical elongation modes for – COOH, respectively.
The microstructures of the as-prepared MOFs were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM). From Fig. 2a and Figure S5a, c, the SEM panoramic image reveals that the Ni-MOF is composed of highly uniform micro-rods of about \(1.8\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) in diameter and \(15\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) in length. With the increasing amount of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) ions added to the solution in the synthesis, the morphologies are changed from the original micro-rods to nanowires (Co/Ni-MOF-2:1, \(700\;\mathrm{nm}\) in diameter and \(26.4~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) in length) (Fig. 2b and Figure S5b, d). The evolution from micro-rods to nanowires might facilitate the electron and electrolyte transport and thus boost the kinetics of redox reactions [42]. The TEM images verify the solid and dense nature of the MOFs (Fig. 2c, d). The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) of the representative Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 reveals its weak crystalline or amorphous nature (Figure S7), which might attribute to the decomposition of the structure under high-power electron beams. Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping images of Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 demonstrate that element distribution patterns match well with high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) image, suggesting that Co atoms are uniformly dispersed throughout the structures (Fig. 2c).
The amount of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) in Co/Ni-MOFs was controlled by the addition of metal salts with different ratios. From inductively coupled plasma-atomic emission spectrometry (ICP-AES) elemental analysis, the actual weight ratios of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) in Co/Ni-MOF-1:2, Co/Ni-MOF-1:1, Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 and Co/Ni-MOF-3:1 are 0.45, 0.77, 1.66, and 2.39, respectively (Table S1). \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms at \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\) demonstrate that Ni-MOF and Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 are nonporous (Figure S9).
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectral analysis was performed to determine the elemental composition and oxidation state of Ni-MOF and Co/Ni-MOF-2:1. The survey spectrum of Ni-MOF displays the characteristic peaks of Ni 2p, S 2p, O 1s and C 1s (Figure S10). Compared with Ni-MOF, Co signal is observed in Co/Ni-MOF-2:1. The high-resolution XPS spectrum of Ni 2p can be deconvoluted into two peaks at \(873.8\ \mathrm{eV}\) and \(856.1\ \mathrm{eV}\) corre
sponding to Ni \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) and Ni \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) orbitals of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\), as well as two shake-up satellites (Fig. 3a) [43]. In the Co 2p XPS spectrum of Co/Ni-MOF-2:1, two peaks and two satellites are observed (Fig. 3b). The peaks at \(797.2~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(781.1~\mathrm{eV}\) are indexed to Co \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) and Co \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) [44]. It was found that there was a wide peak in the range of \(778\sim772~\mathrm{eV}\) in the Co 2p spectrum, which could be because Co 2p peaks were interfered by the NiL Auger peaks [45]. The high-resolution C 1s spectrum can be fitted into three peaks at \(284.7~\mathrm{eV}\), \(285.5~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(288.5~\mathrm{eV}\) assigned to the \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C}\), \(\mathsf{C}{-}\mathsf{O}\) and \(\scriptstyle0-\mathsf{C}=0\), respectively (Fig. 3c) [46]. The high-resolution O 1 s spectrum shows the characteristic peaks at \(531.4~\mathrm{eV}\), \(532.1~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(532.8~\mathrm{eV}\) that can be attributed to the \(\mathbf{M}-\) O \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{N}\mathbf{i}\) or Co), \(\scriptstyle0-\mathsf{C}=0\) and \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\), respectively (Fig. 3d) [37,47].
## 2.2. Supercapacitor
The electrochemical performances of the as-prepared MOFs as supercapacitor electrodes were initially investigated in a three-electrode configuration with a 3 M KOH electrolyte. All the cyclic
voltammogram (CV) curves at a scan rate of \(20\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) show a pair of redox peaks in the potential window of 0–0.6 V (Fig. 4a and Figure S12a), suggesting the redox behavior of the MOFs. For Co/Ni-MOFs, only one pair of redox peaks is observed that is because of the overlapping of the closed redox peaks. This phenomenon has ever been reported in some similar works [43,48]. The peaks may be attributed to the intercalation and deintercalation of \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) during the electrochemical reactions. The possible reaction process can be explained as the follows [49,50]:
\[
\begin{array}{rl}&{\mathrm{Ni}_3(\mathrm{OH})_2(\mathrm{tdc})_2(\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O})_4+\mathrm{OH}^-\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Ni}_3\mathrm{O(OH)}(\mathrm{tdc})_2(\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O})_4}\\&{\quad\quad+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}+\mathrm{e}^-\end{array}
\]
\[
\begin{array}{rl}&{\mathsf{C o}_{\times}\mathsf{N i}_{3-\times}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}(\mathsf{t d c})_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{4}+\mathsf{O H}^{-}}\\&{\quad\quad\leftrightarrow\mathsf{C o}_{\times}\mathsf{N i}_{3-\times}\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{O H})(\mathsf{t d c})_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{4}+\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}+\mathsf{e}^{-}}\end{array}
\]
Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 exhibits the largest integral area and the highest peak current, indicating its superior electrochemical performance. The galvanostatic charge–discharge (GCD) curves of the
electrodes at a current density of \(0.5\;\mathsf{A}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) are presented in Fig. 4b and Figure S12b. The shape of the curves demonstrates the typical pseudocapacitive capacitance, which is in line with the CV curves. Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 shows much longer charge and discharge time than other electrodes. The specific capacitances at different current densities of 0.5 to \(5\;\mathsf{A}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) calculated from the GCD curves are shown in Fig. 4c and Figure S12c. The Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 electrode delivers specific capacitance of \(610\;\mathsf{F}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) at \(0.5\;\mathsf{A}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), which is higher than that of Ni-MOF \((425\;\mathsf{F}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1})\), Co/Ni-MOF-1:2 \((509\;\mathsf{F}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1})\), Co/Ni-MOF-1:1 \((551\;\mathsf{F}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1})\), and Co/Ni-MOF-3:1 \((361\;\mathsf{F}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1})\). The value is comparable to some previously-reported MOF-based electrodes, for example, Co-MOF (CBNWM) [51], Ni-MOF-ADC [52] and Co-MOF-GCE [53], and higher than Co-MOF (CTNBMs) [54], Co/Fe-MOF [55] (Table S2). Additionally, about \(88\;\%\) of the specific capacitance of Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 is retained when the current density increases from \(0.5\;\mathsf{A}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) to \(5\;\mathsf{A}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), which is superior to the monometallic Ni-MOF \((29\;\%\) capacitance retention), suggesting the introduction of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) can greatly enhance the rate performance. Electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) were used to evaluate the reaction kinetics of the MOF electrodes. The corresponding Nyquist plots composed of a semicircular part in high frequency region and an inclined linear part in low frequency region are presented in Fig. 4d. Compared with the Ni-MOF electrode, the smaller semicircle in the high frequency region is observed for the Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 electrode, suggesting its smaller charge transfer resistance. In the low frequency region, the higher slope of the straight line for Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 indicates its faster ion diffusion kinetics. Based on the above analysis, it is clear that Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 possesses lower impedance, which can be attributed to the optimized ratio of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) that will facilitate the electron transfer and the ion diffusion.
The cycling stability is an important parameter to assess the electrochemical performance of electrode materials. The capaci
tance retention of Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 is \(72\;\%\) after 5000 cycles at a current density of \(5\;\mathsf{A}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), which exceeds Ni-MOF \((31\;\%)\), revealing the enhancement of the stability for Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 (Fig. 4e). Notably, the increase of specific capacitance of Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 at the beginning cycles may be because of the gradual permeation of the electrolyte through the electrode and the full activation of the electrode materials. Subsequently, a decrease in capacitance is observed until 1000 cycles, and a stable capacitance retention is achieved upon 5000 cycles. The cycling performance of Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 is better than some reported bimetallic MOF electrodes (Table S2). The stability of Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 was further confirmed by SEM, TEM, XRD and XPS characterizations after the 5000 cycles. The SEM and TEM images show that the morphology of Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 is retained partly and some irregular global substances are generated (Figure S14). The PXRD patterns show the characteristic peaks of Co/Ni-MOF-2:1, \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) (Figure S15), suggesting the coexistence of MOF and metal hydroxides. Additionally, the XPS spectra of Ni 2p and Co 2p exhibit that the peaks \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) are observed (Figure S16), mainly because of the formation of NiOOH and CoOOH [18], respectively. These results are in consistent with the possible charge–discharge mechanism of Co/Ni-MOF-2:1.
Based on the above analysis, the better electrochemical performance of Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 compared to the Ni-MOF could be attributed to the following reasons. First, the introduction of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) with smaller ionic radius \((0.65\;\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) to partially replace the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) \((0.69\;\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) would create more \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) vacancies, which could generate "free holes" in Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 and enhance its electrical conductivity [37,43]. Second, Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 with bimetallic components could introduce more redox active sites to facilitate the reaction. Third, the lower charge transfer resistance and diffusion resistance of Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 could promote the electrochemical kinetics.
To further investigate the detailed energy storage mechanism of the Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 electrode, the CV curves were analyzed for the charge–discharge kinetics. As shown in Fig. 5a, all the CV curves show similar shapes even at high scan rate of \(100~\mathrm{mV~s}^{-1}\). As the scan rate increasing, the anodic and cathodic peaks shift to the positive potential and negative potential, respectively, which is due to the polarization effect. The following equation involving the peak current \((i_{p})\) and scan rate \((\upsilon)\) was used to illustrate the contribution from capacitive and diffusion-control mechanisms: \(i_{p}=a\nu^{b}\) [56,57], where a and b are constants. The value of b can be estimated from the slope of the linear plot from \(\log{(i_{p})}\) versus \(\log(\upsilon)\). In general, the value of b is 1, revealing that a surface capacitive controlled process is dominant, while the b value approaches to 0.5, representing that the diffusion-controlled process is involved. For the Co/Ni-MOF-2:1, the b values of the cathodic and anodic peaks are 0.72 and 0.67, respectively, indicating the mixed charge storage of a capacitive process and a diffusion-controlled process (Fig. 5b). The relative contribution of the two processes to the total charge storage can be quantified based on the following power law: \(i\) \((V)=k_{1}\nu+k_{2}\upsilon^{1/2}\) [56,58], where \(i(V)\) stands for the response current at the potential of V, \(\nu\) is the scan rate, \(k_{1}\nu\) and \(k_{2}\upsilon^{1/2}\) correspond to capacitive-type and diffusion-controlled processes, respectively. A
the value of b is 1, revealing that a surface capacitive controlled process is dominant, while the b value approaches to 0.5, representing that the diffusion-controlled process is involved. For the Co/Ni-MOF-2:1, the b values of the cathodic and anodic peaks are 0.72 and 0.67, respectively, indicating the mixed charge storage of a capacitive process and a diffusion-controlled process (Fig. 5b). The relative contribution of the two processes to the total charge storage can be quantified based on the following power law: i \((V)=k_{1}\nu+k_{2}\upsilon^{1/2}\) [56,58], where \(i(V)\) stands for the response current at the potential of V, \(\nu\) is the scan rate, \(k_{1}\nu\) and \(k_{2}\upsilon^{1/2}\) correspond to capacitive-type and diffusion-controlled processes, respectively. A
typical CV curve at the current of \(10\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) is presented in Fig. 5c, indicating that the capacitive-type process contributes about \(40.1\;\%\) of the total capacity. The ratio of the capacitive-type contribution and the diffusion-controlled contribution at the scan rate from 10 to \(100\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) is shown in Fig. 5d. As the scan rate increasing, the diffusion-controlled contribution gradually decreases due to the limited intercalation of ions into the bulk.
To further evaluate the potential of Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 in practical applications, the asymmetric supercapacitor device (denoted as Co/Ni-MOF-2:1//AC ASC) is assembled using Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 as the positive electrode and active carbon (AC) as the negative electrode, \(3\;\mathrm{M}\) KOH as the electrolyte. The typical CV curves of the Co/Ni-MOF-2:1//AC ASC at the scan rates from 10 to \(100\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) in the potential window of \(0{-}1.4\;\mathrm{V}\) are shown in Fig. 6a. The quasirectangular shapes of the curves indicate the desirable charge match between the positive and negative electrodes. Moreover, there is no evident distortion even at high scan rate, suggesting excellent electrochemical reversibility of the Co/Ni-MOF-2:1//AC ASC device. GCD curves at different current densities from 0.5 to \(5\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) were performed to evaluate the capacitance and the capability of the ASC device (Fig. 6b). Co/Ni-MOF-2:1//AC ASC exhibits a maximum specific capacitance of \(228\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(0.5\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), with \(68\;\%\) of the capacity retention at \(5\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (Fig. 6c), indicating its good rate performance. The cycling performance of the Co/Ni-MOF-2:1//AC ASC was measured at \(5\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), and \(95.5\;\%\) capacitance is retained after 5000 cycles (Fig. 6d), demonstrating its excellent cycling stability.
## 3. Conclusions
In summary, we have successfully synthesized a series of bimetallic Co/Ni-MOFs with different Co/Ni molar ratio by partially substituting \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) in the Ni-MOF with \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\). The synergistic effect of the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) with optimal ratio in Co/Ni-MOF can not only improve the conductivity, but also make the Ni-MOF change from microrods to nanowires that will facilitate ion transport and electron transfer. Compared with Ni-MOF, the Co/Ni-MOF-2:1 exhibits a higher specific capacitance of \(610\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(0.5\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), a better rate performance with \(88\;\%\) capacitance retention at \(5\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and cycling stability with \(72\;\%\) retention after 5000 cycles. Moreover, the Co/Ni-MOF-2:1//AC ASC delivers a high specific capacitance of \(228\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(0.5\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and excellent retention of \(95.5\;\%\) after 5000 circulations at \(5\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). This work demonstrates that the fabrication of bimetallic MOF-based micro/nanosstructures hold great promise for significantly improving the electrochemical performance of MOF-based electrode materials for supercapacitors.
